"""Printed 130–133, vector/scalar functions, limits and continuity."""
from math12_reviewed_higher_derivatives import eq,note
from math12_reviewed_unit1 import heading,paragraph,page,figure
PAGES=[page('069_page_2.jpg',130,[
 heading('Introduction'),paragraph('In the same way that we studied numerical calculus after we learned numerical arithmetic. We can now study vector calculus. Since we already studied vector arithmetic in unit-3 of grade-xi Mathematics. Quite simply, we might have a vector quantity that varies with respect to another variable, either a scalar or a vector. In this unit we shall study the vector functions and the applications of the differential calculus. We shall extend the basic concepts of calculus in a simple and natural way. The study of vector calculus makes the more useful in the geometrical, physical and engineering applications.'),
 heading('5.1 Scalar and Vector Functions'),paragraph('The relationship of calculus and vector methods forms what is called vector calculus. The key to vector calculus is the concept of a vector function.'),heading('i. (a) Scalar Function'),paragraph('A function f(x) is a rule which operates on an input x (x is any scalar quantity) and produces always just a single scalar output y. This gives a proper notation of a scalar function:'),eq(r'y=f(x)','i')
]),page('070_page_1.jpg',131,[
 heading('Scalar function — examples'),paragraph('For example, C(x) = 2x + 2 is a cost function that depends on x number of units of items. Here x is the input, y is the output and C(x) = 2x + 2 is the rule which operates on an input x to produce a single output quantity y. In response of x = 2 items (2 is scalar), the cost (cost is also scalar) is C(2) = 2(2) + 2 = 6 rupees. This function is called a scalar (single variable) function, because it transforms one input x to produce just one output C.'),
 paragraph('A(x,y) = xy is the area of rectangle that depends on length x and width y. Here x,y are the two inputs and the rule A(x,y) = xy operates on two inputs x and y to produce a single output quantity A. In response of x = 2 and y = 1 (2 and 1 are scalars), the area is (is also a scalar) A(2,1) = (2)(1) = 2 square units. This function is then called a scalar (double variables) function, because it transforms two inputs x and y to produce just one output A.'),
 paragraph('This idea can easily be extended to define a scalar multivariate function. The uniqueness of scalar function is to transform scalar quantities in a single scalar quantity. Is there any rule that will transform scalar quantities in a vector quantity? Yes, the rule is the vector functions. Vector functions are used to study curves in the plane and space.'),
 heading('(b) Vector Function'),paragraph('A vector function F⃗ = (f(t),g(t),h(t)) is a function of one variable that has only one “input value”. The “output” values are in two and three dimensional vector spaces instead of simple numbers. In other words, we can say F⃗ is called a vector function of t: F⃗ = F⃗(t).'),
 paragraph('If î,ĵ and k̂ are the unit vectors associated with a rectangular coordinate system (discussed in details in unit-3 of grade-xi), then a vector function F⃗(t) is written as:'),eq(r'F⃗(t)=f_1(t)î+f_2(t)ĵ\quad(2 spaces)'),eq(r'F⃗(t)=f_1(t)î+f_2(t)ĵ+f_3(t)k̂\quad(3 spaces)'),paragraph('We can say that a function F⃗(t) is defined if all its components f₁,f₂ and f₃ are defined.'),
 heading('Example 1'),paragraph(r'Find \(F⃗(\pi/2)\) and \(F⃗(\pi)\) if \(F⃗(t)=\sin(t)î+\cos(t)ĵ\).'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('We have given F⃗(t) = sin(t)î + cos(t)ĵ. As sin(t) and cos(t) are defined for all t, F⃗(t) is defined for all t.'),eq(r'F⃗(\pi/2)=\sin(\pi/2)î+\cos(\pi/2)ĵ=î'),eq(r'F⃗(\pi)=\sin\pi\,î+\cos\pi\,ĵ=-ĵ'),
 heading('ii. Domain and range of a vector function'),heading('a. Domain'),paragraph('The set of all t values used as input in F⃗(t) is called the domain of a vector-valued function F⃗(t).'),heading('b. Range'),paragraph('The set of F⃗(t) values that the vector function F⃗(t) takes as t varies, is called the range of a vector-valued function F⃗(t).'),
 note('The domain is the intersection of the component domains, because all components must be defined simultaneously. Example 1 has domain ℝ and range the unit circle {(sin t,cos t):t ∈ ℝ}, not the whole plane.')
]),page('070_page_2.jpg',132,[
 heading('Example 2'),paragraph(r'Find the domain of (a) \(F⃗(t)=2tî-3tĵ+t^{-1}k̂\); (b) \(F⃗(t)=\sin t\,î+(1-t)^{-1}ĵ+\ln t\,k̂\).'),heading('Solution'),
 paragraph('a. The vector function is:'),eq(r'F⃗(t)=(f_1(t),f_2(t),f_3(t))=2tî-3tĵ+t^{-1}k̂'),paragraph('The function f₁(t) = 2t is defined for all t; f₂(t) = 3t is defined for all t; f₃(t) = t⁻¹ is defined for all values of t except t = 0. Thus the domain is R − {0}.'),
 paragraph('b. The vector function is:'),eq(r'F⃗(t)=(f_1(t),f_2(t),f_3(t))=\sin t\,î+(1-t)^{-1}ĵ+\ln t\,k̂'),paragraph('The function f₁(t) = sint is defined for all t; f₂(t) = (1 − t)⁻¹ is defined for all values except t = 1; f₃(t) = lnt is defined for t > 0. Thus the domain is t > 0,t ≠ 1. The range in each case is of course a vector quantity.'),
 note('The source component sentence in (a) writes f₂(t) = 3t although the vector has −3t; the correct component is −3t, with the same all-real domain. In (b) intersect t > 0 with t ≠ 1 to obtain (0,1) ∪ (1,∞). A range consists of the attained vectors, not all vectors just because the output is vector-valued.'),
 heading('Do You Know? — Operations with vector functions'),paragraph('It follows from the definition of vector operations that vector functions can be added, subtracted, multiplied by a scalar function, and multiplied together. If F⃗ and G⃗ are vector functions of real variable t, and h(t) is any scalar function, then F⃗ + G⃗,F⃗ − G⃗ and F⃗ × G⃗ are vector functions, and F⃗ · G⃗ is a scalar function.'),
 heading('5.2 Limit and Continuity'),paragraph('For the most part, vector limits behave like scalar limits. The proper definition of the limit of a vector function is given below.'),heading('5.2.1 Limit of a vector function and properties of limits of a vector function'),
 paragraph('Let a vector function F⃗(t) be defined for all values of t in some neighbourhood about a point t = t₀ except possibly at t₀ itself, and let L⃗ be a constant vector called limit vector. F⃗(t) approaches L⃗ as t approaches t₀ if for any given real number ε > 0 there is a real number δ > 0 such that |F⃗(t) − L⃗| < ε whenever 0 < |t − t₀| < δ. Symbolically:'),eq(r'\lim_{t\to t_0}F⃗(t)=L⃗'),
 paragraph('Now look at the following useful properties. i. The limit of the sum (difference) of two vector functions is the sum (difference) of their limits. If lim F⃗(t) = L⃗ and lim G⃗(t) = M⃗, where L⃗,M⃗ are constant vector functions, then:'),eq(r'\lim_{t\to t_0}[F⃗(t)+G⃗(t)]=\lim_{t\to t_0}F⃗(t)+\lim_{t\to t_0}G⃗(t)=L⃗+M⃗'),eq(r'\lim_{t\to t_0}[F⃗(t)-G⃗(t)]=\lim_{t\to t_0}F⃗(t)-\lim_{t\to t_0}G⃗(t)=L⃗-M⃗'),
 paragraph('ii. The limit of the dot product of two vector functions is the dot product of their limits. With the same limit vectors:'),eq(r'\lim_{t\to t_0}[F⃗(t)\cdot G⃗(t)]=\lim_{t\to t_0}F⃗(t)\cdot\lim_{t\to t_0}G⃗(t)=L⃗\cdot M⃗'),paragraph('iii. The limit of the cross product of two vector functions is the cross product of their limits:'),eq(r'\lim_{t\to t_0}[F⃗(t)\times G⃗(t)]=\lim_{t\to t_0}F⃗(t)\times\lim_{t\to t_0}G⃗(t)=L⃗\times M⃗'),
 note('In the ε–δ definition, |F⃗ − L⃗| is the vector magnitude (norm), while |t − t₀| is a scalar absolute value. The cross product is the three-dimensional operation; its order matters, unlike a dot product.')
]),page('071_page_1.jpg',133,[
 heading('iv. Scalar-function product limit'),paragraph('The limit of the product of a scalar function and a vector function is the product of their limits. If lim F⃗(t) = L⃗ and lim h(t) = c, where L⃗ is a constant vector and c a scalar constant, then:'),eq(r'\lim_{t\to t_0}[h(t)F⃗(t)]=\lim_{t\to t_0}h(t)\times\lim_{t\to t_0}F⃗(t)=c\times L⃗'),note('Here × between a scalar and a vector denotes ordinary scalar multiplication, not a cross product. The limit is cL⃗.'),
 heading('Example 3'),paragraph(r'Find \(\lim_{t\to2}F⃗(t)\) when \(F⃗(t)=(t^2-3)î+e^tĵ+\sin(\pi t)k̂\).'),heading('Solution'),eq(r'\lim_{t\to2}F⃗(t)=\lim_{t\to2}[f_1(t)î+f_2(t)ĵ+f_3(t)k̂]'),eq(r'=\left[\lim_{t\to2}(t^2-3)\right]î+\left[\lim_{t\to2}e^t\right]ĵ+\left[\lim_{t\to2}\sin(\pi t)\right]k̂'),eq(r'=(4-3)î+e^2ĵ+\sin(2\pi)k̂=î+e^2ĵ,\quad\sin(2\pi)=0'),
 heading('5.2.2 Continuity of a vector function'),paragraph('A vector function F⃗(t) is said to be continuous at t = t₀ if: i. t₀ is in its domain; ii. lim F⃗(t) = F⃗(t₀).'),eq(r'\lim_{t\to t_0}F⃗(t)=F⃗(t_0)'),heading('Do You Know?'),paragraph('A continuous vector value function is also continuous at every point in its domain.'),
 heading('Example 4'),paragraph('For what values of t is F⃗(t) = (sint,(1 − t)⁻¹) continuous?'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The components are f₁(t) = sint,f₂(t) = (1 − t)⁻¹,t ∈ R. f₁ is continuous for all t; f₂ is continuous where 1 − t ≠ 0 (t ≠ 1). Thus F⃗ is continuous when t is a real number other than 1.'),
 heading('Example 5'),paragraph('For what values of t is F⃗(t) = (sint,(1 − t)⁻¹,lnt) continuous?'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The components are f₁(t) = sint,f₂(t) = (1 − t)⁻¹,f₃(t) = lnt,t ∈ R. The first is continuous for all t; the second for 1 − t ≠ 0 (t ≠ 1); the third for t > 0. Thus F⃗ is continuous whenever t is any positive number other than 1: t > 0,t ≠ 1.'),
 heading('History'),paragraph('J. Willard Gibbs was an American scientist. He made his great contributions in the field of mathematics, physics and chemistry. He was the first American who obtained his doctorate degree in engineering after spending three years in Europe, he joined Yale university as professor of mathematical physics from 1871 to his death. He earned international reputation while working in relative isolation. A great scientist Albert Einstein praised him as “the greatest mind in American history”. Together with Oliver Heaviside (Britain and American national) Gibbs developed vector analysis to express the new laws of electromagnetism.'),figure('gibbs-source133','Josiah Willard Gibbs (1839)–(1903)','Original source portrait of Josiah Willard Gibbs in its history box.'),
 note('Continuity is tested component by component on the common domain. The Do You Know sentence refers to a function already known to be continuous; vector-valued output by itself does not imply continuity. The history paragraph is preserved with its source wording.')
])]
PAGES[0]['objectives']=[
 ['5.1 Scalar and Vector Functions',['i. Define scalar and vector function.','ii. Explain domain and range of a vector function.']],
 ['5.2 Limit and Continuity',['i. Define limit of a vector function and employ the usual technique for algebra of limits of scalar function to demonstrate the following properties of limits of a vector function:','The limit of the sum (difference) of two vector functions is the sum (difference) of their limits.','The limit of the dot product of two vector functions is the dot product of their limits.','The limit of the cross product of two vector functions is the cross product of their limits.','The limit of the product of a scalar function and a vector function is the product of their limits.','ii. Define continuity of a vector function and demonstrate through examples.']],
 ['5.3 Derivative of Vector Function',[r'i. Define derivative of a vector function of a single variable and elaborate: if F⃗(t) = f₁(t)î + f₂(t)ĵ + f₃(t)k̂, where f₁,f₂,f₃ are differentiable scalar functions, then \(dF⃗/dt=(df_1/dt)î+(df_2/dt)ĵ+(df_3/dt)k̂\).']],
 ['5.4 Vector Differentiation',[r'i. Prove: \(d a⃗/dt=0\); \(d(F⃗\pm G⃗)/dt=dF⃗/dt\pm dG⃗/dt\); \(d(F⃗\cdot G⃗)/dt=G⃗\cdot dF⃗/dt+F⃗\cdot dG⃗/dt\); \(d(F⃗/\phi)/dt=(\phi dF⃗/dt-(d\phi/dt)F⃗)/\phi^2\); \(d(\phi F⃗)/dt=\phi dF⃗/dt+(d\phi/dt)F⃗\); \(d(F⃗\times G⃗)/dt=F⃗\times dG⃗/dt+(dF⃗/dt)\times G⃗\).','Where a⃗ is a constant vector function, F⃗,G⃗ are vector functions, and φ is a scalar function of t.','ii. Apply vector differentiation to calculate velocity and acceleration of position vector r⃗(t) = x(t)î + y(t)ĵ + z(t)k̂.']]
]
